# =============================================================================
# Docker build context exclusions
# =============================================================================
#
# The Dockerfile builds from the repository root, so without this file the
# entire working tree — including host-platform node_modules, build output and
# any local .env — is sent to the daemon and can overwrite what the image
# installed. Both are correctness problems, not merely size problems.

# --- Dependencies -----------------------------------------------------------
# Critical: these are Windows-host binaries. Copying them over the Linux
# install inside the image produces a broken container.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# --- Build output -----------------------------------------------------------
.next/
**/.next/
out/
**/out/
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/
.vercel/
.turbo/

# --- Caches and compiler artefacts -----------------------------------------
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache
.cache/
coverage/
**/coverage/
.nyc_output/

# --- Version control and CI -------------------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# `tests/config/ci-credentials.test.ts` READS the workflow: it asserts the
# Verify job generates the Prisma client before typecheck, that CI credentials
# carry no dotenv-breaking characters, and that compose validation keeps its
# fail-closed guard. Those assertions are the packaged evidence that CI cannot
# silently regress, so the file they read is a TEST-STAGE INPUT — a suite that
# cannot read its own input is the defect this section exists to prevent, and
# the test must not be weakened to accommodate a missing file.
#
# The exclusions below are DELIBERATELY `dir/*` rather than `dir/`. Docker
# applies the last matching pattern, and a negation on a DIRECTORY re-admits
# everything beneath it: `.github/` followed by `!.github/` re-included the
# whole tree, so a sentinel at `.github/UNRELATED_SENTINEL` and a second
# workflow both reached the build context. Excluding the CONTENTS instead
# leaves each directory traversable while every child stays excluded until
# named, so the policy fails closed for files that do not exist yet — a future
# workflow, an action, CODEOWNERS, an issue template.
#
# Read as a whole: exclude everything in `.github`, re-admit only the
# `workflows` directory so it can be descended into, exclude everything in it,
# then re-admit exactly one file.
.github/*
!.github/workflows/
.github/workflows/*
!.github/workflows/ci.yml

# --- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Real environment files must never enter an image layer. `.env.example` is
# explicitly re-included below: it carries no secrets and documents the
# variables the container expects.
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` are matched relative to the build context ROOT, so they
# exclude `/.env` and nothing deeper. `COPY docker ./docker` therefore carried
# `docker/.env` — the ignored local file holding the real `sa` and application
# passwords — straight into the test image. The `**/` forms below are what
# actually enforce the rule this section always claimed to: no real
# environment file, at any depth, in any layer.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
secrets/
.secrets/

# --- Logs and temporary files -----------------------------------------------
logs/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
*.pid
tmp/
temp/

# --- Test build output ------------------------------------------------------
# The Next build fixture's OUTPUT is a local artefact. The fixture SOURCE is
# deliberately not excluded: the `test` build stage runs the suite and needs
# it. The `dev` runtime stage simply never copies `tests/` at all, which is the
# right place to make that distinction — a stage boundary, not an ignore rule.
tests/fixtures/next-client-boundary/.next/

# --- Authored fixture source in generated-LOOKING directories ---------------
# Round 6 (B2). The build-output patterns above are NAME-based, and the B2
# fixtures are authored source deliberately placed in directories called
# `dist`, `build` and `coverage` to prove the architecture gate no longer
# treats those names as evidence of generated code. Excluding them from the
# build context deleted exactly the files the regression test needs, so the
# Linux suite failed while Windows passed.
#
# These re-includes are narrow: one fixture tree, whose whole purpose is that
# its directory names are misleading. Everything genuinely generated stays
# excluded by the patterns above.
!tests/fixtures/**/src/dist/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/dist/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/build/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/build/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/coverage/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/coverage/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/features/dist/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/features/dist/**
!tests/fixtures/**/src/something/build/
!tests/fixtures/**/src/something/build/**

# Round 7 (B3). This fixture is the opposite case: its `dist/` and `types/` are
# GENUINE build output, checked in on purpose because the defect it reproduces
# is an authored source file importing its own project's emitted declaration.
# A build context that stripped them would leave the import resolving to
# nothing, and the Linux suite would fail where Windows passed — the Round 6
# failure mode exactly.
#
# Scoped to the one fixture path, so every other `dist/` stays excluded.
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/dist/
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/dist/**
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/types/
!tests/fixtures/generated-output-import/apps/app/types/**

# --- Editor and OS junk -----------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Documentation ----------------------------------------------------------
# The frozen architecture documents are not needed to build or run the app.
# Keep them out of the context except for the one baseline the test stage reads.
docs/*
!docs/SECURITY_BASELINE.md

# =============================================================================
# FINAL SECRET DENY — this block must remain LAST
# =============================================================================
#
# Docker applies the LAST matching pattern, so every exclusion above is only as
# strong as the lines that follow it. Two independent defects proved that:
#
#   1. `*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.pfx`, `*.p12` and the certificate patterns are
#      ROOT-ANCHORED. A single `*` never crosses a separator, so they exclude
#      `/server.key` and NOTHING deeper — `apps/web/server.key` and
#      `docker/tls.pem` went straight into the context.
#
#   2. The fixture re-includes above — written for the Round 6/7 architecture
#      fixtures, for reasons that have nothing to do with secrets — reopened
#      `**/.env` beneath themselves. `tests/fixtures/a/src/dist/.env` was
#      admitted by `!tests/fixtures/**/src/dist/**`, the last pattern to match
#      it. Each line was individually correct; their ORDER was not.
#
# This block is therefore the authoritative one, stated recursively and placed
# where nothing above can override it. `scripts/check-docker-context.mjs`
# EVALUATES the whole file against secret paths — including paths derived from
# whatever negations the file happens to contain — so a future edit that
# reopens any of this fails `npm run verify` and CI rather than shipping.
#
# Environment files, at any depth.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Private key and certificate material, at any depth. The root-anchored forms
# above are kept for readability; these are the ones that actually hold.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.pfx
**/*.p12
**/*.crt
**/*.cer
**/*.der

# Credential directories, at any depth.
secrets/
.secrets/
**/secrets/
**/.secrets/

# The ONE deliberate exception (SECURITY_BASELINE.md §18.1): a valueless
# example file, asserted to carry no values by `tests/config/env-example.test.ts`
# and read by the suite inside the `test` stage.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Docker patterns cannot distinguish a file from a directory. Without this
# final descendant guard, a directory named `.env.example` would be re-admitted
# with every secret beneath it. The real valueless file still matches the two
# exceptions above; only content below a same-named directory is denied here.
.env.example/**
**/.env.example/**
